As the authors of the paper note, this demonstrates a significant vulnerability in the safety generalization of large language models. While these models have been shown to be highly effective at generating code that is correct and efficient, they have not been designed to handle the complex and nuanced ethical considerations of code generation. In particular, they may not be able to recognize or reject requests that are harmful or unethical.

To address this vulnerability, the authors propose a new framework called CodeAttack, which transforms natural language inputs into code inputs to test the safety generalization of large language models. By using code inputs, CodeAttack can evaluate the model's ability to recognize and reject requests that are harmful or unethical, and it can provide a more comprehensive assessment of the model's safety generalization capabilities.

